# CLI Documentation Closure — Architecture & Roadmap

## What this is

A roadmap to close the gap between `beaver-cli` (the shipped command surface) and this
repo's `cli/*.mdx` reference — the gap the user named directly in `docs.txt`: *"there is a
huge gap between the docs and the cli commands as I added many cli commands, flags etc and
are yet to be implemented [documented]."* This is a **documentation engineering roadmap**,
not a product feature — it produces `.mdx` pages, an internal coverage inventory, and a
sustaining process. It ships no code.

The thesis, stated as a single testable claim:

> Every `beaver` command and flag that is implemented and usable has a reference page that
> is verified against the source, explains purpose/syntax/consequences/examples/failures,
> and links to the task guide that uses it — while every internal mechanism (orchestration,
> routing, schemas, storage, the deployment-contract format) stays out of Mintlify entirely.

Formally, **V1 is: a coverage inventory with zero unresolved rows, a command reference page
for every implemented, usable command family, a shared-flags reference, task guides linking
reference pages into workflows, `mint broken-links` clean, and a documented review step
wired into how commands/flags change going forward.**

## Why this, why now

`docs.txt` establishes two things the user wants held simultaneously, in tension:
1. **Disclosure discipline** — "I do not want to expose internals and things that copycats
   can use against me." Reinforced by this repo's own `CLAUDE.md`: matching logic,
   infrastructure-provisioning mechanics, and anything resembling the internal
   deployment-contract schema are trade secrets and must never appear in public docs.
2. **Completeness** — every command and flag the user shipped should be "explained
   thoroughly": purpose, syntax, every flag's defaults/interactions, consequences, examples,
   results and failures.

These are not actually in conflict. Rule 6 of the writing guide (`docs.txt`, echoed as
Invariant 6 below) is the hinge: document **observable behavior** — what a user can do, see,
and expect — never the mechanism that produces it. `--wait`'s timeout behavior is public;
the job system behind it is not. This roadmap treats that line as a first-class design
constraint, not an afterthought applied at review time.

---

## Current state — the actual inventory (audited against `beaver-cli` source, 2026-09-17)

`beaver-cli/commands/` has **~65 top-level command groups** across 458 Go files. This repo's
`cli/` has **46 `.mdx` pages**. The overlap is smaller than 46 suggests: several existing
pages are stale (`plan.mdx`/`plans.mdx` predate the current model per `CLAUDE.md`'s note on
historical drift) or map to a *subcommand* of a still-undocumented group rather than a group
of their own (`logs.mdx`/`metrics.mdx`/`traffic.mdx` are `observability` subcommands;
`login.mdx`/`logout.mdx`/`whoami.mdx` are `auth` subcommands; `contract.mdx`/`validate.mdx`
map to `projectcontract`/`config validate`).

**Zero-coverage groups, sized by flag-definition call sites (a proxy for documentation
effort) and subcommand count:**

| Group | Subcommands | Flag sites | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `service` | 46 (incl. `database`, `cluster`, `backup-destination` nested groups) | 37 | **Largest undocumented surface.** No `cli/service.mdx` exists at all. |
| `cloud` | 31 | 34 | **Second largest.** No `cli/cloud.mdx` exists at all. |
| `config` | 27 (incl. `bind`, `network`, `volume`, `builder`, `schema`) | 55 | Highest flag density of any group. |
| `statecmd` | 7 (`apply`/`drift`/`history`/`pull`/`rollback`/`status`) | 8 | **High disclosure risk** — see Invariant 6 note below. |
| `projectcontract` | 9 (`plan`/`diff`/`push`/`pull`/`rollback`/`status`/`validate`/…) | 4 | **High disclosure risk.** |
| `autoscale` | — | 8 | No page. |
| `preflight` | — | 13 | No page. |
| `admin` | 3 | 3 | Operator-facing; classification question (Era 10). |
| `mystic`, `internal`, `mcpcmd`, `skillscmd`, `envbanner`, `initcmd`, `route`, `reconcilecmd`, `promote`, `projectconfig`, `projectcontext`, `integration`, `health`, `coupon`, `ui` | small | small | Long tail; several may not belong in public docs at all (Era 10). |

**`alie`** has 56 files / 41 `Use:` sites / 22 flag sites — the single largest raw command
tree — and an existing `alie.mdx`, but at that scale the page is almost certainly thin
relative to the surface. Audited separately in Era 11 alongside `ai`/`mcpcmd`/`skillscmd`
because the AI surface carries its own disclosure question (agent tool wiring, MCP
transport) distinct from infrastructure commands.

This table is the *headline*, not the full inventory — Era 1 (V1–V4) produces the complete,
row-per-command-and-flag inventory this roadmap actually executes against. The table above
is enough to sequence the work: **service and cloud first** (biggest, zero coverage, core to
the product), **config/state/contract families last among "normal" work** (highest
disclosure risk, needs the templates and rules settled first), **AI surface and admin/
internal long tail get their own risk-classification pass** rather than blanket "document
everything."

---

## Layering — who owns what

| Layer | Owns | Changes here |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `beaver-cli` (source of truth) | the actual command tree, flags, defaults, help text | **Nothing.** This roadmap is read-only against the CLI. If a command's behavior looks unfinished or contradicts its help text, that is a CLI bug, filed separately — not silently documented as-is. |
| Internal engineering docs (outside this repo — `beaver-cli/CLAUDE.md`, `vhb-server` internal docs) | orchestration, routing, schemas, the deployment-contract format, job-system internals | **Nothing new required.** If a command's *why* needs explaining and the honest answer is internal, that explanation goes there, not in Mintlify — Mintlify explains the observable contract only. |
| This repo (`mintlify`) | `cli/*.mdx` reference pages, task guides, `docs.json` nav | Everything this roadmap produces. |
| `/features/docs/` (this roadmap, planning-only) | the coverage inventory, the family grouping, the version plan, the gap log | Not published, not linked from `docs.json`, not part of the site build — an internal planning artifact, same role as `beaver-desktop/features/*`. |

---

## Invariants

Ten, taken directly from the ten writing rules the user and assistant agreed on in
`docs.txt` — restated as constraints every version below must satisfy, not just the
writing eras:

1. **Verify before describing.** Every claim in a page is checked against registered
   commands/flags/implementation in `beaver-cli`, not against help text or memory of what a
   command "probably" does.
2. **Cover every command and flag.** The coverage inventory (Era 1) is the single source of
   truth for what remains; nothing is marked done until every flag — including aliases,
   accepted values, defaults, requirements, conflicts, interactions — is on the page.
3. **Purpose before syntax.** Every command page opens with the problem it solves and when
   to reach for it, before the syntax block.
4. **Consequences are explicit.** What changes locally vs. remotely, whether a restart or
   redeploy follows, whether the action reverses, what happens on failure or timeout — stated
   for every mutating command.
5. **Examples are adaptable, never real.** One consistent set of fictional
   projects/services/domains across all pages (defined in Era 2). No real credentials,
   machine IDs, or infrastructure identifiers, ever.
6. **Observable behavior only — this is the disclosure boundary.** Inputs, outputs,
   limitations, recovery steps: yes. Database schemas, private endpoints, routing/placement
   algorithms, the reconcile/contract engine, orchestration internals: never, regardless of
   how directly a command like `beaver state drift` or `beaver projectcontract plan` invites
   the question "how does it decide?" The page answers "what will I see," not "how is it
   computed."
7. **Reference and guides are separate, linked.** Command reference pages (per-family,
   Eras 3–11) exhaustively cover options. Task guides (Era 12) connect commands into an
   outcome. Every reference page links to the guide(s) that use it; every guide links to the
   reference pages it exercises.
8. **Shared flags, one home.** Global/repeated flags (`--wait`, `--yes`, `--json`,
   `--machine`, `--quiet`, etc. — enumerated in Era 2) get one canonical explanation; each
   affected command page links there and states only its local deltas.
9. **Validate the finished page, not just the draft.** Navigation entry, links,
   `mint broken-links`, syntax highlighting, and — critically — whether each example was
   actually executed against a running `beaver` or only reviewed against source. The two are
   marked differently (Era 13).
10. **Docs are part of the change, going forward.** A command/flag/default/output change in
    `beaver-cli` triggers a documentation review before or alongside merge, not as a
    periodically-rediscovered backlog. Era 13 is where this becomes a standing process
    instead of a one-time cleanup.

---

## The mental model this roadmap teaches (page structure)

Every command reference page (any family, Eras 3–11) follows the same shape, so the
inventory in Era 1 doubles as a page checklist:

```
Frontmatter (title, description, sidebarTitle, icon)
      │
Purpose            ← what problem, when to reach for it (Invariant 3)
      │
Syntax & arguments ← required inputs, accepted identifiers, defaults
      │
Flags              ← every flag: meaning, values, default, conflicts, example
      │              (shared flags link out — Invariant 8)
      ▼
Consequences        ← local/remote effect, redeploy?, reversible? (Invariant 4)
      │
Examples             ← common usage, realistic combinations (Invariant 5)
      │
Results & failures  ← reading output, common errors, recovery
      │
      ▼
Linked task guide(s) ← where this command is used in a workflow (Invariant 7)
```

---

## Data model

**None.** This roadmap produces `.mdx` files, a `docs.json` nav diff, and planning artifacts
under `/features/docs/`. No schema, no database, no new component. The one piece of
"state" is the coverage inventory itself (Era 1 V4) — a checked-in artifact under
`/features/docs/inventory/`, not part of the published site.

---

## Phase / Era / Version index

~47 versions, 13 eras, 4 phases.

### Phase 1 — Ground truth

| Era | File | Versions | Theme |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | `01-command-inventory-and-audit.docs` | V1–V4 | Extract the full command/flag tree, cross-reference against existing `cli/*.mdx`, classify maturity, publish the inventory |
| 2 | `02-disclosure-boundary-and-templates.docs` | V5–V7 | Codify the disclosure checklist, the page template, the shared-flags reference, the fictional-example vocabulary |

### Phase 2 — Reference build-out, by family, ordered by size and risk

| Era | File | Versions | Theme |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 3 | `03-identity-session-and-account.docs` | V8–V10 | `auth` (login/logout/whoami), `account`, `billing`, `coupon` |
| 4 | `04-connect-machine-and-health.docs` | V11–V14 | `connect`, `machine`, `doctor`, `agent`, `health`, `recover` — the onboarding spine |
| 5 | `05-deploy-and-containers.docs` | V15–V18 | `deploy`, `compose`, `container`/`containers`, `docker`, `deployment`, `deployui`, `promote` |
| 6 | `06-infrastructure-pools.docs` | V19–V22 | `cloud`, `scaling`, `autoscale`, `edge`, `domain`, `firewall` |
| 7 | `07-services-and-data.docs` | V23–V26 | `service` (+ `database`, `cluster`, `backup-destination`), `integration`, `github` |
| 8 | `08-observability-and-diagnostics.docs` | V27–V29 | `observability` family (logs/metrics/traffic/latency/active/live), `observe`, `history`, `status`, `inspect`, `diff` |
| 9 | `09-config-state-and-contracts.docs` | V30–V33 | `config`, `state`/`statecmd`, `projectconfig`, `projectcontext`, `projectcontract`, `route`, `reconcilecmd` — **highest disclosure risk era** |
| 10 | `10-admin-preflight-and-platform-ops.docs` | V34–V36 | `admin`, `preflight`, `mystic`, `envbanner`, `initcmd`, `internal` — classify public vs. internal-only before writing anything |
| 11 | `11-ai-surface.docs` | V37–V39 | `ai`, `alie`, `skillscmd`, `mcpcmd` — disclosure pass on agent/tool/MCP wiring |

### Phase 3 — Guides and integration

| Era | File | Versions | Theme |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 12 | `12-workflow-guides-and-navigation.docs` | V40–V43 | Task guides (deploy, connect+machine-roles, scaling, rollback/recover), `docs.json` nav wiring, cross-linking, reconciling the naming mismatches found in Era 1 (`plan`/`plans`, `logs`/`metrics`/`traffic` vs. `observability`, `login`/`logout`/`whoami` vs. `auth`) |

### Phase 4 — Verify and sustain

| Era | File | Versions | Theme |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 13 | `13-verification-and-sustaining-process.docs` | V44–V47 | `mint broken-links`, verified-vs-reviewed example audit, the CLI-change → docs-review gate, the recurring inventory refresh |

**The gating era is Era 1.** Nothing in Phase 2 starts family-by-family writing until the
inventory (V1–V4) exists and the templates/rules (V5–V7) are fixed — otherwise each family
reinvents the disclosure line and the page shape independently, which is exactly the
drift `docs.txt` is trying to stop.

---

## Explicitly not in this roadmap

- **No internal architecture, orchestration, routing, or schema docs, anywhere, ever** —
  not even in a "for internal reference" section of a public page. That content's home is
  `beaver-cli`/`vhb-server`'s own internal docs, outside this repo.
- **No rewriting `blog/` or `changelog/`.** `CLAUDE.md` marks those as intentionally-left
  historical records of the prior product model.
- **No CLI code changes.** If the audit (Era 1) finds a command that is genuinely
  unfinished (stub, hidden, error-on-call), it is excluded from "supported usage" docs and
  logged as a gap — this roadmap does not fix the CLI.
- **No new Mintlify components.** Every page uses the existing catalog (`components.txt`).
- **No screenshots or recordings of the product's internal dashboards** unless the task
  guide is specifically about the Web/GUI surface, which is out of scope here (CLI only).

---

## Acceptance gate (V47)

This roadmap is done when:

1. The coverage inventory (Era 1) has zero rows in "unresolved" status — every command and
   flag is one of: documented-fully, documented-with-stated-limitations, or
   excluded-with-a-logged-reason (placeholder/internal-only/removed).
2. Every family in the Phase 2 table has a published `cli/*.mdx` page (or page set, for
   `service`/`config` where one page would be unreasonably long) matching the Era 2 template,
   with every flag covered per Invariant 2.
3. Every shared flag is documented once and linked from every page that uses it — no flag's
   behavior is explained twice with a risk of drifting.
4. `mint broken-links` is clean across `cli/` and the new task guides.
5. Every code example is marked either "verified: executed against a live `beaver`" or
   "reviewed against source" — none are unmarked.
6. A reviewer can grep any published `cli/*.mdx` page for orchestration/routing/schema/
   internal-API language and find none — spot-checked against the Invariant 6 checklist for
   every family, with the Era 9 and Era 11 families getting a second, explicit pass.
7. `docs.json`'s `cli` nav group reflects every published page; no page exists that isn't
   reachable from navigation, and no nav entry points at a stale or removed command.
8. The Era 13 process doc exists and is referenced somewhere a future command/flag change
   would actually be seen (e.g. linked from `beaver-cli`'s own `CLAUDE.md` or CONTRIBUTING
   equivalent) — so the gap this roadmap closes does not reopen.

---

## Sustaining process (Era 13 V46–V47)

Two mechanisms keep this roadmap's result from silently decaying, reinforcing each other:

**Per-change gate (V46).** `beaver-cli/CLAUDE.md` has a "Keeping the public CLI reference in
sync" section (added 2026-09-17, right before its `## Architecture` section) with the
concrete checklist: does this change a command's syntax/flags/defaults/output, does the
matching `mintlify/cli/<page>.mdx` still match, does a new command have a page, and a
pointer back to `DISCLOSURE.md` for the disclosure-sensitive families. This is the thing a
human or an AI assistant changing a command actually reads, since it's that repo's own
`CLAUDE.md` — not a rule living only here where a `beaver-cli` contributor would never see
it.

**Periodic refresh (V47).** The per-change gate catches drift *when someone remembers to
apply it*; it won't catch a flag renamed in a large refactor that didn't think to touch docs,
or slow accumulated drift across many small changes. Cadence: **re-run the Era 1 extraction
(`features/docs/inventory/extract_commands.py` → `cross_reference.py` → `build_summary.py`)
against the then-current `beaver-cli` every time it ships a minor version, or quarterly,
whichever comes first.** Diff the regenerated `commands.csv`/`doc_status.csv` against the
checked-in versions; any new command, removed command, or changed flag set is the punch list
for that refresh's doc updates. No automation is built for this in V1 — a manual, periodic
re-run is sufficient to start (see `inventory/00-summary.docs`'s note on why re-deriving
statistically would be worse than direct source reads for the trickier cases). Automating the
extraction's execution (not its judgment calls) is the natural next step if the manual
cadence proves to lag — tracked as a future improvement, not built here.

Both mechanisms are recorded in two places on purpose: this file (so a future session picking
up this roadmap finds the whole sustaining story in one place) and `beaver-cli/CLAUDE.md`
(so a `beaver-cli` contributor sees the actionable half without needing to know this repo
exists).
